PyHealth
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`clinicalbench/_vendor/pyhealth/` contains a reduced copy of PyHealth v1.1.4.

    https://github.com/sunlabuiuc/PyHealth
    Copyright (c) 2022 Sunlab @ UIUC
    Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License.

    Yang, C., Wu, Z., Jiang, P., Lin, Z., Gao, J., Danek, B., and Sun, J.
    "PyHealth: A Deep Learning Toolkit for Healthcare Predictive Modeling."
    KDD 2023.

ClinicalBench uses PyHealth only to read the MIMIC-III and MIMIC-IV tables and
to derive the three prediction tasks. The copy here is vendored rather than
installed from PyPI so that cohort construction stays pinned to the exact code
that produced the published results.

Modifications made to the vendored copy:

  1. Removed every subsystem ClinicalBench does not use: `models/`, `calib/`,
     `metrics/`, `interpret/`, `sampler/`, `unittests/`, `trainer.py`,
     `tokenizer.py`, `medcode/pretrained_embeddings/`, the non-MIMIC dataset
     backends (eICU, OMOP, MIMIC-Extract, and the signal datasets), and the
     task modules other than length-of-stay, mortality and readmission.

  2. Rewrote internal absolute imports from `pyhealth.*` to
     `clinicalbench._vendor.pyhealth.*`, so installing ClinicalBench cannot
     shadow a real PyHealth installation in the same environment. (Before this
     change the project's own `setup.py` declared `name = "pyhealth"` and
     `pip install .` would replace an installed PyHealth.)

  3. Trimmed `datasets/__init__.py` and `tasks/__init__.py` to the symbols that
     survive the prune, and removed `__main__` demonstration blocks in the three
     retained task modules that referenced the removed dataset backends.

No functional change was made to cohort construction, code mapping, or task
definitions. Downloaded code-mapping resources are still cached under
`~/.cache/pyhealth/`, shared with an upstream install if one is present.
